1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:07,944
Malika has a question for the bank, and it is a fair one: why does she pay more per train set than the shop next door?

2
00:00:07,994 --> 00:00:12,312
Dilnoza can answer it in one minute, and this lesson is why.

3
00:00:12,362 --> 00:00:26,424
In Lesson 2, the acquirer kept its fee and paid Malika the rest, and in Lesson 21, each website got its own deal, and this lesson decides that fee, for every website and every card.

4
00:00:26,474 --> 00:00:32,592
The easy way is to write the fee straight into the payment code, one rate for everyone.

5
00:00:32,642 --> 00:00:40,128
Then one merchant agrees a lower rate, one website gets a special offer, and one card costs the bank more.

6
00:00:40,178 --> 00:00:43,848
Soon the code is a tangle nobody dares touch.

7
00:00:43,898 --> 00:00:46,320
So the bank uses fee rules.

8
00:00:46,370 --> 00:00:49,872
A fee rule is one price the bank sets.

9
00:00:49,922 --> 00:00:55,152
It can apply to one website, one merchant, one card, or the whole bank.

10
00:00:55,202 --> 00:01:01,376
So when two rules fit one payment, which one should win?

11
00:01:01,426 --> 00:01:05,480
When a payment arrives, the most specific rule wins.

12
00:01:05,530 --> 00:01:09,176
A website's own rule beats its merchant's rule.

13
00:01:09,226 --> 00:01:12,272
A merchant's rule beats a card's rule.

14
00:01:12,322 --> 00:01:17,168
And the bank's default is used only when nothing more specific applies.

15
00:01:17,218 --> 00:01:22,976
The shop next door has its own merchant rule, and Malika pays the bank's default.

16
00:01:23,026 --> 00:01:27,248
The fee itself is a percent of the amount, plus a fixed part.

17
00:01:27,298 --> 00:01:30,080
It is kept between a minimum and a maximum.

18
00:01:30,130 --> 00:01:35,456
Money is always counted in whole cents, never in fractions of a cent.

19
00:01:35,506 --> 00:01:41,504
So the percent is rounded once, to whole cents, and the sum is exact.

20
00:01:41,554 --> 00:01:46,952
Otherwise small fractions would leak away across millions of payments.

21
00:01:47,002 --> 00:01:49,232
Dilnoza opens Fees.

22
00:01:49,282 --> 00:01:57,632
The page says it plainly: the most specific rule wins, and a new rule applies after a second admin approves it.

23
00:01:57,682 --> 00:02:03,752
Filters show the rules in force now, the ones waiting for approval, or all of them.

24
00:02:03,802 --> 00:02:08,120
This month the bank's default is changing, so she clicks New rule.

25
00:02:08,170 --> 00:02:14,000
Under "Applies to" there are three choices: merchant, website and card.

26
00:02:14,050 --> 00:02:18,320
A hint says to leave everything empty for the bank default.

27
00:02:18,370 --> 00:02:25,088
If she picked a merchant but no card, the form would say a merchant or website rule needs a card.

28
00:02:25,138 --> 00:02:32,360
She leaves the scope empty, sets a percent, a fixed part and a minimum, and clicks Send for approval.

29
00:02:32,410 --> 00:02:35,432
The page says another admin has to approve it.

30
00:02:35,482 --> 00:02:41,816
Here is the rule I would defend hardest: rules are never edited in place.

31
00:02:41,866 --> 00:02:49,880
A change is a new rule, proposed by one admin and approved by a second, the four eyes from Lesson 18.

32
00:02:49,930 --> 00:02:54,824
In Approvals, under Fee changes, Dilnoza's own buttons are grey.

33
00:02:54,874 --> 00:02:59,576
A colleague signs in, opens the same tab, and clicks Approve.

34
00:02:59,626 --> 00:03:06,200
On Fees, the rule now shows as in force, with who proposed it and who approved it.

35
00:03:06,250 --> 00:03:14,096
Two rules for the same thing can never apply at the same moment: the database itself refuses an overlap.

36
00:03:14,146 --> 00:03:15,968
Now Malika's question.

37
00:03:16,018 --> 00:03:19,064
Dilnoza opens the Fee calculator.

38
00:03:19,114 --> 00:03:22,592
Its hint says it shows what a payment would pay right now.

39
00:03:22,642 --> 00:03:27,800
She chooses Malika's shop and her website, because a quote needs both.

40
00:03:27,850 --> 00:03:30,224
The amount is already filled in.

41
00:03:30,274 --> 00:03:32,096
She clicks Calculate.

42
00:03:32,146 --> 00:03:37,784
The answer shows the fee, the rule used, and how much Malika receives.

43
00:03:37,834 --> 00:03:43,952
And every payment keeps the rule it used, its fee, and what the merchant receives.

44
00:03:44,002 --> 00:03:51,152
So if Malika asks next year about last March, the answer is still there, whatever the rules say today.

45
00:03:51,202 --> 00:03:58,040
To recap: the most specific fee rule wins, and every change needs a second admin.

46
00:03:58,090 --> 00:04:02,632
So which rule does Malika's shop pay?

47
00:04:02,682 --> 00:04:06,880
The bank's default, because nothing more specific applies.

48
00:04:06,930 --> 00:04:11,272
Next: the virtual card machine each payment goes through.
